<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Li Cunxu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/885">885</a>–<a href="/years/926">926</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Emperor Zhuangzong of Later Tang</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Li Cunxu was the son of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shatuo">Shatuo</a> Turk warlord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-keyong">Li Keyong</a> and his successor as Prince of Jin from 908. A gifted soldier, he repeatedly defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/later-liang">Later Liang</a>, annexed the Yan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-shouguang">Liu Shouguang</a> in 913 and took most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebei">Hebei</a>. In 923 he proclaimed himself emperor of the Later Tang and destroyed the Later Liang the same year. His favour to actors and neglect of his army led to a mutiny in which he was killed in 926.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/li-cunxu.jpg" alt="Li Cunxu" width="320" height="676" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zhuangzong_of_Later_Tang.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Li Keyong's son <b>Li Cunxu</b>, succeeding in 908, defeated the Liang at Bochao in 911, annexed the Yan (Five Dynasties) of Liu Shouguang in 913 and won most of Hebei.</blockquote><a href="/state/jin-five-dynasties">Jin (Five Dynasties)</a></li><li><blockquote>Jin steadily took Hebei, and in 923 <b>Li Cunxu</b>, now ruler of the Later Tang, captured Kaifeng; Zhu Youzhen had himself killed.</blockquote><a href="/state/later-liang">Later Liang</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Li Cunxu</b>, ruler of Jin (Five Dynasties) at Taiyuan, proclaimed himself emperor of a restored Tang in 923 and within months captured Kaifeng, ending the Later Liang.</blockquote><a href="/state/later-tang">Later Tang</a></li><li><blockquote>When <b>Li Cunxu</b> destroyed the Later Liang and founded the Later Tang, Li Maozhen submitted in 924 and was confirmed as Prince of Qin.</blockquote><a href="/state/qi-five-dynasties">Qi (Five Dynasties)</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Li Cunxu</b> of Jin (Five Dynasties) sent his general Zhou Dewei against Yan in 911.</blockquote><a href="/state/yan-five-dynasties">Yan (Five Dynasties)</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiyuan">Taiyuan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.87,112.54&amp;year=911" aria-label="Taiyuan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7357 802 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Li Cunxu"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7807 1072h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7807 1072h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.87,112.54&amp;year=911">What was here in 911 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
